    "result_text": "AcceptanceTest is the only test class. The full build passes. Let me verify the build compiles cleanly (no unused-import issues) which BUILD SUCCESS already confirms.\n\nThe change is complete. Here's a summary:\n\n## Summary\n\nI changed telephone handling to store and compare numbers in **E.164 form**. All tests pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 3/3 in `AcceptanceTest`).\n\n### Changes\n\n**`TelephoneNormalizer.java`** \u2014 rewrote `normalize()` to produce E.164:\n- Detects a leading `+` (explicit country code) after trimming.\n- Strips all non-digits (spaces, dashes, brackets).\n- If no `+`: assumes country code `61` and drops a single leading `0` from the national digits.\n- Requires 8\u201315 digits after the `+`; returns `null` otherwise (\u2192 400).\n- Result is `\"+\" + digits`, e.g. `0412 345 678` \u2192 `+61412345678`, `+64 21 123 456` \u2192 `+6421123456`, `12` \u2192 rejected.\n\nThe controller (`OwnerRestControllerV1`) already stored and compared duplicates via `normalize`, so it now stores and dedupes on E.164 values with no change needed there.\n\n**`Owner.java`** \u2014 replaced the field constraints (`@Digits(integer=10)` + `@Pattern ^[0-9]{10}$`) with `@Pattern ^\\+[0-9]{8,15}$`, so the stored E.164 value passes JPA/Bean Validation (this was causing the initial 500 `ConstraintViolationException`). Removed the now-unused `@Digits` import.\n\n**`openapi.yml`** \u2014 updated the `telephone` field description to document the E.164 normalization (the schema's `minLength: 1`/`maxLength: 20` already accommodates raw input and the E.164 output).\n\nThe DB column is `VARCHAR(20)`, which comfortably fits E.164's max 16 characters. Existing 10-digit seed data still works: it's normalized on the fly during duplicate comparison.",
